WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.890
EDR is powerful, but it is not magic.

00:00:02.890 --> 00:00:06.230
It can be misconfigured, ignored, bypassed, overwhelmed by alerts,

00:00:06.230 --> 00:00:08.260
or limited by poor coverage.

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:11.815
If endpoints are unmanaged, offline, excluded, or not sending telemetry,

00:00:11.815 --> 00:00:13.460
defenders may have blind spots.

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:17.885
EDR works best when it is deployed broadly, tuned carefully, monitored consistently,

00:00:17.885 --> 00:00:20.060
and connected to incident response processes.

00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.860
Good practices include maintaining endpoint coverage, reviewing high-value alerts,

00:00:23.860 --> 00:00:26.985
tuning noisy detections, documenting response procedures,

00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:29.420
integrating with SIEM or SOAR where appropriate,

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.060
and training analysts to investigate endpoint behavior.

00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:34.920
EDR is one layer in a defense-in-depth strategy.

00:00:34.920 --> 00:00:38.965
It does not replace patching, identity security, network monitoring, backups,

00:00:38.965 --> 00:00:42.590
user awareness, secure configuration, or incident response planning.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:46.430
Its value comes from reliable visibility, useful detections, practiced response,

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:47.400
and human judgment.
